#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063830 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8edb0a6c-f22f-56cf-9545-2fae93896db2" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Continuity at a Point Using the Definition"}] :question/id #uuid "2b7ec4d6-260d-5fcd-bde2-cb84052868cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-10705" :question/problem "Consider the following function:\n${1 + x,\\begin{vmatrix}x \\le 1 \\\\ 1 - x\\end{vmatrix}x > 1$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $f(x)$?\n\n1. $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$\n2. $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^-} f(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 1^+} f(x)$\n3. $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1} f(x)= f(1)$\n4. $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n1. Does $f(c)$ exist?\n2. Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n3. Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x) = f(c)$?\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Using the expression for the first branch of the function, we obtain that\n$f(1)= 1+1= 2$.\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$. $\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n- Statement II is false. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n$lim_(x → 1^{-})f(x)|= lim_(x → 1^{-})(1 + x); = 1 + 1; = 2; lim_(x → 1^{+})f(x)|= lim_(x → 1^{+})(1 - x); = 1 - 1; = 0$\nAs a result:\n$\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^-} f(x) \\neq \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^+} f(x)$\nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ does not exist. $\\:\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n- Statement III is false since the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ does not exist. $\\:\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n- Statement IV is false. Since not all statements I-III are true, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/314/Source/Images/dfa809445d41802185fca6d9c871b1a0.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063831 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa1ddb40-3494-541c-b23e-b15dd98bf669" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reason for a Discontinuity"}] :question/id #uuid "a9fce66e-f0e3-50a6-a2d9-0407b9d91d5e" :question/math-academy-id "e-10706" :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x=2$?\n${3x + 2,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 2 \\\\ 6,\\end{vmatrix}x = 2; x^{3}, \\mid x > 2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2$.\n\n- $f(2) = 6$, so $f(2)$ exists. ${\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and find that they coincide:\n$lim_(x → 2^{-})f(x)|= lim_(x → 2^{-})(3x + 2); = 3(2) + 2; = 8,; lim_(x → 2^{+})f(x)|= lim_(x → 2^{+})(x^{3}); = 2^{3}; = 8$.\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and $\\lim_\\limits{x\\to 2} f(x)=8.\\, \\, {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$ $\\ \\[1ex]$\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: $\\lim_\\limits{x\\to 2} f(x)= 8$, while $f(2) = 6$. ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2$ because\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to 2} f(x) \\neq f(2)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063832 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70029065-69c3-585d-a6a1-cc9d2485a60a" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Continuity to Evaluate a Limit"}] :question/id #uuid "8f95e034-a0fe-5535-9a25-ce549f29c9d1" :question/math-academy-id "e-10707" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$ and $f(2)=2$, then what is the value of\n$\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x\\to 2} \\sqrt{x^2-2f(x)}}\\,$?" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$. According to the definition of continuity, this means that $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 2} f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2} f(x)=f(2)=2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x\\to 2} \\sqrt{x^2-2f(x)}}=\\sqrt{2^2-2\\cdot 2} = 0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8edb0a6c-f22f-56cf-9545-2fae93896db2" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Continuity at a Point Using the Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2711c71-6149-551a-9acf-33f3f3099dda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061247} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d57359-cff3-50fe-afd5-499a71d46ad9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061248} {:answer/id #uuid "48532c79-2864-5249-a7ae-e10014e73a3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061249} {:answer/id #uuid "4e8c73ab-11df-5711-b3d6-ab8d3f8cc940" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061250} {:answer/id #uuid "6153f19f-38b4-5c2e-8e1f-dfdff6d6619c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48532c79-2864-5249-a7ae-e10014e73a3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061249} :answer-field/id #uuid "079004be-e3b9-5b05-a9d9-8f196ec198da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061246}] :question/id #uuid "9d96aa7c-3228-53f8-9efa-38bbf767192d" :question/math-academy-id "q-152832" :question/problem "Consider the following function:\n$f(x) = {2x - 1,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 1 \\\\ 2 - x,\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 1$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $f(x)$?\n\n1. $f(x)$ is defined at $x = 1$\n2. $lim_(x → 1^{-})f(x) = lim_(x → 1^{+})f(x)$\n3. $lim_(x → 1)f(x) = f(1)$\n4. $f(x)$ is continuous at $x = 1$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064669 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70029065-69c3-585d-a6a1-cc9d2485a60a" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Continuity to Evaluate a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "927989fa-3ca9-535f-8667-2d7f8b8dc4c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062526} {:answer/id #uuid "9f41ec2e-7382-50fe-8c29-da9f1349dda7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062527} {:answer/id #uuid "cd12c798-6560-5ce6-ad17-8f7f85453db5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062528} {:answer/id #uuid "df3eb747-84d0-58f8-8318-efa046a4ffe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062529} {:answer/id #uuid "e6f9c91b-bd39-51ad-8524-e48c0d7b4c92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f41ec2e-7382-50fe-8c29-da9f1349dda7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062527} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a7066f7-a989-5894-895f-42ffb2a7e5ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062525}] :question/id #uuid "c4de1a86-cf32-5ec4-aebe-3bc6f5b68dec" :question/math-academy-id "q-38450" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x =-2$ and $f(-2) = 2$, then what is the value of\n$lim_(x → (-2))((f(x))^{2} + 4)$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064723 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa1ddb40-3494-541c-b23e-b15dd98bf669" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reason for a Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80e4863d-dded-580a-aef2-6a3d29d03756" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ is a piecewise function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062850} {:answer/id #uuid "e7782bc4-9cd0-5561-9785-561eefe94631" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$lim_(x → 3^{+})f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062851} {:answer/id #uuid "207754ad-d4b0-54ee-9d18-145671f8cdbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$lim_(x → 3)f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062852} {:answer/id #uuid "271161a3-9817-5e8a-91ce-2a1b6768ddca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(3)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062853} {:answer/id #uuid "683231ca-3f33-5bcc-bafa-de17af8a392d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "lim_(x → 3)f(x) \\ne f(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "683231ca-3f33-5bcc-bafa-de17af8a392d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "lim_(x → 3)f(x) \\ne f(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062854} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7cc255c-086f-5bda-8a24-0fedbbc29dee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062849}] :question/id #uuid "8fd6c9be-8ceb-56ab-a994-cb6b2495af35" :question/math-academy-id "q-48053" :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x = 3$?\n$f(x) = {x^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}x < 3 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}x = 3; 2x + 3, \\mid x > 3$"}]}
